# Neo Transient parameters (docs/specs/mastering-tools.md §5.4). The order is permanent: songs and presets
# store values by position. Regenerate: python3 tools/gen-machine-params.py Machines/NeoTransient
namespace NeoBuzz.Transient
class NeoTransientMachine
summary Transient shaper for drums, buses and masters: Attack and Sustain gains (±12 dB) on the onset and the body of what comes in, found by two followers of its level (Punchy, Balanced or Smooth), with an optional lookahead so the gain is already there at the first sample. Impact mode splits into four bands (zero-latency Linkwitz–Riley crossovers) and moves each band's microdynamics (its short-term level against its long-term level) with one bipolar knob, with auto gain per band. Left/right or mid/side, Mix, Delta (hear only what it changes) and a gain-match reading. Use it on a drum bus for snap or tail control, and on a master in small moves.

group Transient
Attack     "Attack"       0 240   120   db -12 12                          | Gain on the onsets (the first milliseconds of a hit), 0.1 dB steps. 0 dB with Sustain at 0 dB passes the audio bit for bit
Sustain    "Sustain"      0 240   120   db -12 12                          | Gain on the body and the tail after the onset, 0.1 dB steps
Speed      "Speed"        0 2     1     enum Punchy Balanced Smooth        | The followers' time constants. Punchy: onsets of 0.1 ms, fast release. Balanced: the default. Smooth: slower, for bass and long hits
Lookahead  "Lookahead"    0 50    30    lin 0 5 ms 1                       | The audio is delayed this long so the gain is already up at the first sample of a hit (reported to NeoBuzz Live and compensated; 0 is no latency, and the first millisecond of a hit then gets less of the Attack gain)
group Impact
Mode       "Mode"         0 1     0     enum Attack_Sustain Impact         | Attack/Sustain: the two gains above. Impact: four bands, each with its microdynamics moved by its Impact knob (Attack and Sustain are not used)
Xo1        "Crossover 1"  0 10000 2594  freq 20 20000                      | Between the first and the second band (Impact mode)
Xo2        "Crossover 2"  0 10000 5663  freq 20 20000                      | Between the second and the third band (never below Crossover 1)
Xo3        "Crossover 3"  0 10000 8257  freq 20 20000                      | Between the third and the fourth band (never below Crossover 2)
Impact1    "Impact 1"     0 1000  500   bip                                | The first band's microdynamics: −100 % takes them out (the band follows its slow level), +100 % doubles them (in dB), 0 leaves them
Impact2    "Impact 2"     0 1000  500   bip                                | The second band's microdynamics
Impact3    "Impact 3"     0 1000  500   bip                                | The third band's microdynamics
Impact4    "Impact 4"     0 1000  500   bip                                | The fourth band's microdynamics
Auto       "Auto gain"    0 1     1     switch                             | Impact mode: each band's energy stays what it was whatever its Impact (a 300 ms average, within ±12 dB)
group Output
Stereo     "Stereo"       0 1     0     enum L/R M/S                       | What is shaped: left and right (one gain for both, from the louder), or mid and side (each its own)
Mix        "Mix"          0 1000  1000  pct                                | Dry/wet (the dry signal is delayed to match)
Output     "Output"       0 480   240   db -24 24                          | Gain after the shaper, 0.1 dB steps (the editor's Match sets it from the gain-match reading)
Delta      "Delta"        0 1     0     switch                             | Hear only what the shaper changes: the output minus the input, delayed to match (gliding over 10 ms)

# What the shaper measures (NeoTransientMachine.Readings, machine_readings), over the last 3 s.
reading gain_match_db     dB    | Short-term loudness out − in (K-weighted, 3 s): what Output would have to take back for equal loudness
reading gain_max_db       dB    | The largest gain applied (to one band, in Impact mode)
reading gain_min_db       dB    | The smallest gain applied (negative: the most it took away)
reading transient_percent %     | How much of the time the audio was in an onset (Attack/Sustain mode)
reading latency_samples   samples | The lookahead, reported to NeoBuzz Live
